static const char* const kAboutRef = "<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\"><b>Physics Lab</b></div><div class=\"lrnSm\">A playground for seeing how the universe works: real orbits, atoms, black holes and a little space game. It is built on real physics, and where something is simplified, it says so.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The developer:</b> Physics Lab is made by <b>Alex</b>, one idea and one update at a time. If you find a bug or have an idea, tell Alex.</p><p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Built with Claude:</b> Alex decides what goes in and comes up with the ideas. It is built together with Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, which helps write the code, check the facts and test everything.</p><div class=\"lrnP srcBox\"><b>Where the facts come from:</b> planet positions come from NASA/JPL orbital data, and the isotope table is based on the NUBASE nuclear data (through the mendeleev package). Facts in the guides are checked against several sources, and where the sources disagree the text says so. The main ones:<div class=\"srcH\">🪐 Space and orbits</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/approx_pos.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: planet positions (Keplerian elements, 1800–2050)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: planetary fact sheet</a> (sizes, masses, gravity, temperatures)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: Small-Body Database</a> (dwarf planets, asteroids, comets)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/phys_par/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: planetary satellite physical parameters</a> and <a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/elem/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">mean elements</a> (the moons: sizes and orbits)</li></ul><div class=\"srcH\">⚛️ Atoms and particles</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://www-nds.iaea.org/amdc/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NUBASE and the Atomic Mass Evaluation</a> (isotopes, half-lives, binding energy)</li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1088/1674-1137/abddae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NUBASE2020 (Kondev et al., 2021)</a> (how each unstable nucleus decays and the odds of each way, for radioactive decay in the Atom Lab)</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/lmmentel/mendeleev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The mendeleev Python package</a> (the data table the isotopes came through)</li><li><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Particle Data Group</a> (quark masses)</li><li>Wikipedia: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proton\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Proton</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neutron\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Neutron</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomic_nucleus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Atomic nucleus</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bohr_model\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bohr model</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomic_orbital\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Atomic orbital</a></li><li><a href=\"https://zenodo.org/record/2493915\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Niels Bohr, 1913: On the constitution of atoms and molecules, Part I</a> (the 1913 paper behind the Bohr 1913 style)</li></ul><div class=\"srcH\">🕳️ Black holes</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.03808\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">James, von Tunzelmann, Franklin and Thorne, 2015: Gravitational lensing by spinning black holes in astrophysics, and in the movie Interstellar</a> (how light is traced around a spinning black hole)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerr_metric\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the Kerr metric</a> (the spacetime of a spinning black hole)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innermost_stable_circular_orbit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: innermost stable circular orbit</a> (where the bright disc ends)</li></ul><div class=\"srcH\">🌀 Spacetime and real objects</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schwarzschild_metric\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the Schwarzschild metric</a> (the spacetime of a round, non-spinning object; Flamm’s paraboloid, the real shape of space, is explained here)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interior_Schwarzschild_metric\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the interior Schwarzschild metric</a> (the spacetime inside an object of the same density everywhere)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schwarzschild_geodesics\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Schwarzschild geodesics</a> (the paths the marbles follow, circular orbits and the last steady orbit)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_time_dilation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: gravitational time dilation</a> (why clocks tick slower deeper in gravity)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.07674\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">IAU 2015 Resolution B3</a> (the official nominal mass and radius of the Sun)</li><li><a href=\"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/moonfact.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Moon fact sheet</a> (mass and radius; Earth’s are in the planetary fact sheet above)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.00880\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Fonseca et al., 2021: Refined mass and geometric measurements of PSR J0740+6620</a> (the heavy neutron star, 2.08 Suns)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09091\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Miller-Jones et al., 2021: Cygnus X-1 contains a 21-solar mass black hole</a> (Science)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.07478\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">GRAVITY Collaboration, 2022: mass distribution in the Galactic Centre</a> (Sagittarius A*, 4.3 million Suns)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.11238\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Event Horizon Telescope, 2019: First M87 results I, the shadow of the black hole</a> (M87*, 6.5 billion Suns)</li></ul><div class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The 1.4-Sun, 12 km neutron star is a typical textbook example, not one particular star, and the 70 kg human and 0.6 kg basketball are round numbers.</div><div class=\"srcH\">🌌 Stars and far-away things</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://github.com/astronexus/hyg-database\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HYG star database v4.1 (David Nash, astronexus)</a> (the stars and their colours, built from the Hipparcos, Yale and Gliese catalogues; licence CC BY-SA 4.0, changed here into the ecliptic coordinates of the planets)</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/Stellarium/stellarium\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Stellarium: the “modern” constellation lines</a> (licence CC BY-SA 4.0)</li><li><a href=\"https://theskylive.com/voyager1-info\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Sky Live: Voyager 1</a></li><li><a href=\"https://theskylive.com/voyager2-info\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Sky Live: Voyager 2</a> (their sky positions and distances on 30 Sep 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Voyager 1</a></li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Voyager 2</a> (speeds, and the dates they reached the Sun’s wind edge)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heliosphere\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the heliosphere</a> (where the Voyagers crossed the heliopause: 121 AU in 2012 and 119 AU in 2018)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha_Centauri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Alpha Centauri</a> (4.34 and 4.24 light years)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pleiades\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the Pleiades</a> (Gaia distance, 444 light years)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orion_Nebula\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the Orion Nebula</a> (1,267 light years)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sagittarius_A*\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Sagittarius A*</a> (27,000 light years)</li></ul><div class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Distances of Polaris, Betelgeuse and Rigel are uncertain, so they are rounded.</div><div class=\"srcH\">🌍 Spinning planets and dates</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10569-017-9805-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Archinal et al.: Report of the IAU Working Group on Cartographic Coordinates and Rotational Elements, 2015</a> (the direction of each planet’s north pole and how fast it turns)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.sai.msu.ru/neb/Bidon/ChaptersPDF/nsd_App06b8_Rot.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The IAU rotational elements table (PDF)</a> (Mars to Pluto, checked against the numbers below)</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/CS-SI/Orekit/blob/develop/src/main/java/org/orekit/bodies/PredefinedIAUPoles.java\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Orekit: the IAU pole and turning models as code</a> (Mercury, Venus, Earth and the small wobble terms of Mars and Neptune)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axial_tilt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: axial tilt</a> (to compare each planet’s lean)</li><li><a href=\"https://starchild.gsfc.nasa.gov/docs/StarChild/questions/question60.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA StarChild: Mars in August 2003</a> (27 Aug 2003, 55.76 million km)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_conjunction\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: great conjunction</a> (Jupiter and Saturn, 21 Dec 2020, 0.1° apart)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_transit_of_Venus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the 2012 transit of Venus</a></li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halley%27s_Comet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Halley’s Comet</a> (closest on 9 Feb 1986, due back 28 Jul 2061)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discovery_of_Neptune\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the discovery of Neptune</a> (23 Sep 1846)</li></ul><div class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Planet places on other dates come from the NASA/JPL orbit numbers listed under Space and orbits.</div><div class=\"srcH\">☀️ The Sun, seasons and the timeline</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://github.com/CS-SI/Orekit/blob/develop/src/main/java/org/orekit/bodies/PredefinedIAUPoles.java\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Orekit: the IAU models as code</a> (the Sun’s pole, 286.13° and 63.87°, and its turning rate, 14.1844° a day)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_rotation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: solar rotation</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">the Sun</a> (the equator turns faster than the poles; the axis leans 7.25°)</li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Earth facts</a> (the 23.4° tilt, and that the tilt, not the distance, causes the seasons)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apsis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: apsis</a> (Earth’s closest and farthest distance from the Sun, with the 2026 dates)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solstice\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: solstice</a> (the 2026 equinox and solstice times)</li><li>Saturn’s ring: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christiaan_Huygens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Christiaan Huygens</a> and <a href=\"https://dwc.knaw.nl/en/?p=2435\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">the Huygens ring discovery (KNAW)</a></li><li>The Sun is not the centre of the galaxy: <a href=\"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Harlow-Shapley\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Britannica: Harlow Shapley</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galactic_Center\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Galactic Center</a></li><li>Landing on Venus and Mars: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venera_7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Venera 7</a>, <a href=\"https://solarviews.com/eng/venera7.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Solarviews: Venera 7</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viking_1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Viking 1</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/viking-1/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Viking 1</a> (the sources disagree on how long Venera 7 transmitted, so the text gives no number)</li><li>A comet hits Jupiter: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comet_Shoemaker%E2%80%93Levy_9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9</a> and <a href=\"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Comet-Shoemaker-Levy-9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Britannica</a></li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Voyager 1</a> (leaving the Sun’s wind, 25 Aug 2012, 121 AU)</li><li>Comet landing and Ceres: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosetta_(spacecraft)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Rosetta</a>, <a href=\"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/rosettas-philae-makes-historic-first-landing-on-a-comet/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: Philae’s landing</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dawn_(spacecraft)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Dawn</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dawn/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Dawn</a></li><li>Touching the Sun: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parker_Solar_Probe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Parker Solar Probe</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/parker-solar-probe/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Parker Solar Probe</a> (the corona crossing was on 28 Apr 2021 and announced in Dec 2021)</li><li>Weighing the black hole: <a href=\"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2020/press-release/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S2_(star)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the star S2</a></li></ul><div class=\"srcH\">🧪 Everyday element facts</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_chemical_elements\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the element pages</a> (uses, melting points, abundance)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abundance_of_the_chemical_elements\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: abundance of the elements</a> (what the universe and Earth’s crust are made of)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/107536-highest-electrical-conductivity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Guinness World Records: highest electrical conductivity</a> (silver)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iridium_anomaly\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the iridium layer</a> (the asteroid that ended the dinosaurs)</li></ul></div><p class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Version: Physics Lab 8.6.2. Labs: Orbit Lab 7.7.2 · Atom Lab 7.7.3.</p>";
static const char* const kLangRef[] = {"<span style=\"font-size:18px\">🇬🇧</span><span class=\"nm\">English</span><span class=\"ck\">✓</span>", "<span style=\"font-size:18px\">🇪🇪</span><span class=\"nm\">Eesti keel</span><span class=\"soon\">Coming soon</span>"};
static const char* const kLangLabelRef = "🌐 English";
static const char* const kHistoryRefTitle = "Physics Lab · history";
static const char* const kHistoryRef[] = {"<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.6.2 · you are here</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Unlock everything (for testing)</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Settings has a new Developer section with one button, Unlock everything. It unlocks every secret theme and every achievement at once, with no pop-ups, so they are easy to test. The Reset my progress button above it locks them all again.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.6.1</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Bigger table without scrolling</div><div class=\"lrnD\">The ⤢ Bigger table pop-up is now wide enough to show the whole periodic table at once, with no sideways scroll bar. On a phone the buttons shrink to fit.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.6</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Tidy-up: readable, bigger, closer</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Four small fixes from a design review. The facts written on top of the picture now sit on a soft see-through panel, so they stay readable even over bright clouds and stars. The periodic table has a new ⤢ Bigger table button that opens it large, with buttons big enough for a finger, scrolled to the element you have loaded. The small grey text all over the app is one step bigger (Settings still scales it). And in the Atom Lab the zoom buttons (Atom, Nucleus, Nucleon, Centre), Drag and Reset view now sit right under the picture instead of in a card further down.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.5.1</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Reduced motion</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Settings has a new Motion choice: Auto, Reduced or Full. Reduced is for anyone who feels dizzy or distracted when a lot moves on screen. Labs start paused with a ▶ Play button, camera moves jump straight to the end instead of gliding, Cloud 3D does not spin by itself, the Did you know facts change only when you tap them, and pop-ups appear without sliding or fading. The physics stays exactly the same. Auto (the default) follows the device’s own “reduce motion” setting. Your choice is remembered between visits.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.5</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Unit switch</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Settings has a new Units choice: Metric or Imperial. It changes the distances, speeds and temperatures the labs work out: planet, moon, comet and star cards in the Orbit Lab, its scale bar, the Black Hole readout, element melting and boiling points, the room temperature line and atom speeds in the Atom Lab, and the size comparisons in the Atom Lab Scale guide. Science units (nm, K, kg) and the written guides stay as they are. Your choice is remembered between visits.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.4.6</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Spectrum, decay history and lighter orbits</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Atom Lab 7.5 brings a Light spectrum card, a Decay history card and half-lives in the periodic table. Orbit Lab 7.7.1 draws its trails with less work and looks exactly the same.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.4.5</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Half-Life achievement</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A new achievement: watch an unstable nucleus decay in the Atom Lab. Radioactive decay arrived in Atom Lab 7: every unstable nucleus now decays on its own at its real half-life.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.4.4</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Smoother frames in every lab</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Every frame, the app used to rewrite the hint text and the buttons and re-read the theme colours, even when nothing had changed, which made the browser lay out the whole page again 60 times a second. Now it only touches the page when something really changes, and reads the colours only when you change the theme. Also in this update: Orbit Lab 7.7 (faster at high time speed) and Black Hole Lab 3.3 (spin labels fixed, lighter when paused) and Spacetime Lab 2.6.1 (a crash with heavy neutron stars fixed).</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.4.3</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Help guides on one page</div><div class=\"lrnD\">The Orbit, Atom and Spacetime Lab help guides are no longer dropdown boxes. Each is one long page with its headings kept, a row of tappable headings at the top to jump around, and a small ↑ on every heading to come back.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.4.2</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Learn tab keeps you on the Learn tab</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Tapping a guide or timeline in the 📚 Learn tab now opens it right there instead of switching you into that lab. Close it and you are back on the Learn list, in the lab you were in before.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.4.1</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Sources for the Sun, seasons and the new timeline steps</div><div class=\"lrnD\">The About page now lists where the Orbit Lab’s newest facts come from: the Sun’s real spin, why Earth has seasons (with the 2026 equinox and solstice times), and each of the new Solar System timeline steps, from Huygens and Saturn’s ring to the 2020 Nobel Prize for weighing the black hole at the centre of our galaxy.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.4</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Galactic Center achievement</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A fourth achievement: click the black hole at the centre of our galaxy (Sagittarius A*) in the Orbit Lab. Zoom a very long way out, or tap it under Far away, and it is yours.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.3</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Sources for the Orbit Lab’s spinning planets</div><div class=\"lrnD\">The About page now lists where the Orbit Lab’s spinning planets and its Jump to a date come from: the IAU report on how each planet turns and leans, and the pages behind the famous dates (Mars in 2003, the 2020 great conjunction, the 2012 transit of Venus, Halley’s Comet and the discovery of Neptune).</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.2</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Sources for the Orbit Lab’s stars</div><div class=\"lrnD\">The About page now credits the star data behind the Orbit Lab’s new night sky and far-away things (the HYG star database and Stellarium’s constellation lines, both CC BY-SA 4.0) and lists the sources for the Voyagers and the far-away objects.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8.1</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Sources for the Spacetime Lab</div><div class=\"lrnD\">The About page now lists the scientific sources behind the Spacetime Lab: the papers and pages for the Schwarzschild spacetime, the shape of space, the marble paths and clock slowing, and the measurements of the real objects (the Sun, the Moon, the heavy neutron star, Cygnus X-1, Sagittarius A* and M87*).</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 8</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Spacetime Lab</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A fourth simulation: gravity as bent spacetime. A grid shows the bend around a star, a black hole, a planet, a Moon, a human or a basketball, and you can roll marbles across it to see them circle, fall in or fly by. A Bend setting switches between Easy to see and True proportions (a human makes no dent at all), and the readout shows each object’s real mass, size, horizon radius and escape speed. A guide explains what everything is and what the picture gets wrong, and it is in the Learn tab too (Spacetime Lab 1).</div>|▶ Open Spacetime Lab",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 7.6</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Buttons instead of drop-down menus</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Every setting that used to open a drop-down menu is now a row of buttons you can always see, with the one in use highlighted: the electron pictures in the Atom Lab (Orbit, Cloud, Cloud 3D, Slice), Orbit style, quark style, On and Off switches in every lab, Quality in the Black Hole Lab, and more. A small note under the row explains the chosen option. The lab picker at the top and the Famous atoms list in the Atom Lab are still menus, because their lists are longer.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 7.5</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Learn tab, Learn cards higher up</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A new 📚 Learn tab sits next to Simulations, Games and Achievements. It lists every guide and timeline (the Atom Lab’s guide, Scale, Weight and Timeline, and the Orbit Lab’s guide and Timeline). Tap one and it opens in its lab. The Learn card in the Orbit Lab and the Atom Lab also moved up: it is now the third card in the Orbit Lab (under Time speed) and the second card in the Atom Lab (under the periodic table). On phones the four tabs now sit in two rows so none is cut off.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 7.1</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Clearer progress reset</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Settings → Progress → Reset my progress now opens a pop-up page. It says this is permanent and lists exactly what will be locked again (for example 2 secret themes and 3 achievements). After you confirm, a second pop-up says Progress reset with what was locked, and the Settings page shows a highlighted Done line. If you have nothing unlocked yet, it tells you there is nothing to reset.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 7</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Achievements</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A new 🏆 Achievements tab next to Simulations and Games shows every achievement, locked until you earn it. When you earn one, a small pop-up slides in at the corner of the screen for a few seconds and goes away by itself, so it never blocks what you are doing. The first three: Neon Nights (find the secret Synthwave theme), Sharp Eyes (find the secret Monochrome theme) and Point of No Return (zoom all the way in on a black hole). Settings → Progress now locks the achievements again too. Also new: the About page lists sources for the Black Hole Lab, the moons and Bohr’s 1913 paper.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 6.9</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Atom Lab: Bohr 1913 style, plus many fixes</div><div class=\"lrnD\">The Orbit view has a new Orbit style setting. “Bohr 1913” draws the atom the way Niels Bohr drew it: one small nucleus ball with a plus sign, the fixed circles n = 1 to 4, and one electron ball. No neutrons, no quarks. ↓ Emit photon and ↑ Absorb photon make the electron leap between circles with a flash. The Modern style is still there. Also fixed the photon label running off the edge of the picture on phones. Also in this update: the Black Hole Lab got a Quality setting (Auto, High, Medium and Low, so phones run smoother) and rendering fixes (a dotted line through the picture, a hairline seam, a broken patch when looking down on the disc; the Viewing angle now starts at 20°). The Orbit Lab lets go of a planet without a jump, zooms deep more smoothly (the Ceres lag is fixed), starts every belt view the same way, and has short tails with a new Trails setting. Slider knobs now always drag, and the update list no longer shows “undefined” on phones.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 6.8</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">FPS counter, exact comet orbits, progress reset and Orbit guide</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Every lab now shows its frames per second in the top right corner of the picture. In Orbit Lab the comets stay exactly on their orbit line at any zoom (before, they slid off it and seemed to bounce when zoomed far in). Settings has a new Progress part with a Reset button that locks the secret themes again, after an “are you sure” step. I also timed both Orbit Lab and Atom Lab across all their views, zoom levels and time speeds and tried random taps and drags: no other slow parts or errors turned up. Orbit Lab now has the same ❓ guide as Atom Lab. It explains in plain words what every ring, dot, line and colour on the picture means, what is to scale and what is not, what Time speed means, why the Sun wobbles, and how comets get their tails. It also shows live facts about what you have open and selected.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 6.7</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Orbit Lab: scale bar, famous comets, no lag near the Sun, and a secret theme</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A scale bar with light travel time, and six famous comets on their real orbits with glowing heads and tails: Halley, Hale–Bopp, NEOWISE, Encke, 67P and Swift–Tuttle. A new secret colour theme hides somewhere in the app. It stays a ??? in the 🎨 Theme list until you find it. Clue: read the Atom Lab guide closely. Zooming in close to the Sun (or anywhere the comets' dotted orbits run far off screen) made the app crawl. Only the part of each comet orbit that is on screen is drawn now, so it stays smooth.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 6.6</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Orbit Lab: real-looking planets, Sun and true scale</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Planets, moons and the Sun became lit balls, then smooth single-colour balls with a soft glowing Sun that spins. The Orbit Lab now starts at 1 s = 29 days, and moons follow the Time speed properly. A new Barycentre marker shows the real balance point of the Sun and planets, which the Sun wobbles around. True scale: planets, moons and the Sun are drawn at their real size, zoom goes up to ×300,000, Earth has real continents and clouds, and there is no minimum dot any more (bodies smaller than a pixel are marked with a small ring). A Sizes setting brings back the bigger planets. The Time speed slider moved up, right under Reset to today.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 6.5</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Orbit Lab: Go to table</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A new Go to card in Orbit Lab with a tile for the Sun, every planet, dwarf planet and moon, and the belts and the far edge. Tap one and the view flies there. Trails behind far-away bodies, the dwarf planets most of all, no longer lag behind and jump: they always reach the body. The note at the top left of the picture now shows the real date as time passes, for example 29 Sep 2126 after 100 years. Going to a planet, moon or belt no longer changes the time speed you chose. The mouse wheel zooms in smaller steps in Orbit Lab, so far-away dwarf planets are easier to reach. The ring around the selected planet, moon or Sun is now bright pink with a dark edge. Belts and the Oort cloud light up in pink with a name tag when you go to them. Tap a selected Go to tile again to deselect it. Orbit Lab now has the little x y z axes at the bottom right, like Atom Lab.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 6.4</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Atom Lab: steadier quarks, sharper info button, element card</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Fixed the quark triplets that flickered, disappeared and reappeared when you looked closely at a nucleus. Quark triplets no longer appear and disappear out of thin air in big nuclei: they fade in and out smoothly as you turn or move the picture. The ⓘ info button in Atom Lab is now a sharp, drawn icon, a little bigger and easier to tap. Every element now has a card under the periodic table: its tile, family, period and group, state at room temperature, and a plain-words line about where you meet it in daily life.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 6.3</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Language, About, Settings and sources</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A new 🌐 button in the header lets you pick a language: English works now, and Estonian is marked “Coming soon” (the translation is next on the planned list). A new 👤 About button tells you who makes Physics Lab, what it is built with and where the facts come from. A new ⚙ Settings button in the header. It has a Text size setting (small, normal, large, extra large) and a Font setting (theme default, serif, easy to read, rounded, monospace). Both change the text across the whole app, including the labels drawn inside the labs, and are remembered. More settings can go there later. The About page now links to the main sources behind Physics Lab’s facts: NASA/JPL for the planets, NUBASE and the Particle Data Group for atoms and particles, and Wikipedia and other pages for the everyday element facts.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 6.2</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Atom Lab: “What am I even looking at?” and every view draggable</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A new ❓ button in Atom Lab explains the atom on screen in plain words, with sections you can open and close: nucleus, protons, neutrons, quarks, the electron, and what each picture and number means. It follows the element you pick. All four electron views can be dragged now. Orbit turns in 3D, Cloud slides around, Slice moves its cut through the cloud, and Cloud 3D rotates as before. Fixed a bug where the electron cloud seemed to disappear: moving the picture and then zooming in could push it off screen. It now always stays in view.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 6.1</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Orbit Lab: moons stay visible</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Fixed a bug where moons (Earth’s Moon, the moons of Mars and others) disappeared at some angles in the 3D view.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 6</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Colour themes and the “Did you know?” line</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A line at the top of the screen shows a physics fact and swaps to a new one every few seconds. Tap it to skip to the next fact. There are 25 facts about space, temperature and atoms. A new 🎨 Theme button in the header opens a short list: Auto (follows your device), Light, Dark, Black, Midnight, Solar and Paper. Your choice is remembered on this device. All labs use the new colours. Sage (a calm, cool grey-green) and High contrast (black with white text and yellow accents) join the 🎨 Theme list. The list also scrolls now when the screen is short, such as a phone held sideways. The fact line now shows which fact you are on (like 3/26) and waits while you hold it with your finger or hover over it with the mouse, so you can finish reading. There is one new fact too, and it is a surprise. There is a new locked slot at the bottom of the 🎨 Theme list, and now 27 facts in the 💡 line. Keep reading the facts to find out how to open it.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 5.6</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Weight panel</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A new Learn panel in Atom Lab about weight: what an atom and its parts weigh, the missing mass and binding energy, how many atoms are in everyday things, why a proton’s weight is mostly not quarks, and a mass ladder from the electron to the Sun (Atom Lab 5.6).</div>|▶ Open Atom Lab",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 5.5</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Temperature in the Atom Lab</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Every element shows its melting and boiling point, state at room temperature and density. A temperature slider shows atoms as a solid, liquid or gas (Atom Lab 5.5).</div>|▶ Open Atom Lab",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 5.1</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Physics Lab history</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A timeline of the app itself, opened from the 🕰 button next to the title. It lists every big step so far, with a ▶ button that jumps to the lab or game it is about, and what is planned next.</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 5</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Orbit Lab: the real Solar System</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Orbit Lab was simplified to just the real Solar System, placed where everything is today. It got a 21-step timeline from wandering stars to DART, and time speed that goes down to real time (Orbit Lab 2.5).</div>|▶ Open Orbit Lab",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 4.6</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">A realistic nucleus, and phone fixes</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Protons and neutrons are placed like a real nucleus (random, non-overlapping, neutron skin, measured deformation such as uranium’s rugby-ball shape). Shorter readout with an ⓘ button, and long text wraps on phones in every lab (Atom Lab 5).</div>|▶ Open Atom Lab",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 4.5</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Learn panels</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Atom Lab gained a scale comparison (“if the nucleus were a football …”), a powers-of-ten ruler and a 22-step timeline of how we pictured the atom, with buttons that jump to the matching atom (Atom Lab 4.5).</div>|▶ Open Atom Lab",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 4</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">The first game: Slingshot</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A game inside the lab: launch a spacecraft from Earth on a real date, aim by dragging, and reach a planet with limited fuel. Six missions from Venus to Neptune’s orbit, a quiz for each, stars for fuel saved, and a Simulations / Games switch at the top (Slingshot 1).</div>|▶ Play Slingshot",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 3.5</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Save image</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A 📷 Save image button in every lab saves exactly what you see as a PNG (Atom Lab 4.1).</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 3</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Black Hole Lab</div><div class=\"lrnD\">The third lab: a black hole with a glowing accretion disc that bends light, drawn on the GPU (with a slower fallback), and then spin, using the equations for a rotating Kerr black hole (Black Hole Lab 1–3).</div>|▶ Open Black Hole Lab",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 2.5</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">The periodic table and isotopes</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Atom Lab: click any of the 118 elements, with real half-lives for all 3,557 known isotopes, X-rays for heavy atoms and realistic quarks with colour charge (Atom Lab 3.5–4).</div>",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 2</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Atom Lab</div><div class=\"lrnD\">A second lab appeared, so a lab menu was added. It began with a nucleus and one electron on an orbit, then grew a probability cloud, a rotatable 3D view, a zoom down to the nucleus and a zoom into the quarks (Atom Lab 1–3). Each lab now shows its own Update number, and clicking it opens that lab’s changelog. Your settings are remembered between visits, with a Defaults button.</div>|▶ Open Atom Lab",
  "<div class=\"lrnY\">Physics Lab 1</div><div class=\"lrnTt\">Physics Lab is born</div><div class=\"lrnD\">Orbit Lab: a star with planets you can add and launch, then a 3D view and click-for-info on anything. Then the real planets from NASA/JPL data with sizes, masses and gravity, real moons, the asteroid belt, Kuiper belt, dwarf planets and the Oort cloud (Orbit Lab 1–2).</div>"};
