static const FarNoteRef kFarNoteRef[] = {{"acen", "The nearest star system: two Sun-like stars circling each other."}, {"prox", "The closest star to the Sun: a small, dim red dwarf."}, {"barn", "A dim red dwarf that crosses the sky faster than any other known star."}, {"sirius", "The brightest star in our night sky."}, {"lal", "A red dwarf, too faint to see without a telescope."}, {"eri", "A star a little cooler than the Sun, with a disc of dust around it."}, {"procyon", "One of the brightest stars in the winter sky."}, {"cyg61", "Two orange stars circling each other."}, {"tau", "A Sun-like star that is close enough to study closely."}, {"altair", "A fast-spinning white star, one of the three bright stars of the Summer Triangle."}, {"vega", "A bright white star, one of the brightest in the northern sky."}, {"fom", "A bright star with a ring of dust and rocks around it."}, {"arct", "A bright orange giant star."}, {"pol", "The North Star. Its distance is uncertain, about 430 light years."}, {"bet", "A huge red star in Orion, about 500 light years away (the distance is uncertain)."}, {"rig", "A blue-white supergiant in Orion, about 860 light years away (the distance is uncertain)."}, {"pleia", "A cluster of young blue stars born together."}, {"m42", "A cloud where new stars are being born."}, {"sgra", "The black hole at the centre of our galaxy, about 4.3 million Suns (see the Spacetime Lab)."}};
static const char* const kLookSkyRef[8] = {"<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The stars in the background</b> (the <b>Soft</b> setting keeps them dim while you look at the planets and brightens them as you zoom out; <b>Full</b> keeps them bright) are real: the 2,874 brightest ones (about what your eyes see in a dark place), each in its true direction and true colour, with a soft band for the flat disc of our galaxy, the Milky Way (its real plane, drawn as a simple glow, not a photograph). They are so far away that they do not move when you zoom or follow something. They only move when you turn the picture in the 3D view (in 2D you look straight down, so you see the half of the sky below the Solar System).</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Constellations</b> (in the Sky card) draws the lines people drew between stars long ago. The stars of a constellation are usually not near each other: they only look that way from here.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Far-away things:</b> zoom out a very long way (or tap a tile under Far away). The <b>white rings with a coloured dot</b> are real stars at their real direction and distance, like Alpha Centauri, the nearest star system, 4.3 light years away. The distance rings count light years too. The <b>blue rings</b> are the Pleiades and the Orion Nebula, and the <b>ring with a black dot</b> is Sagittarius A*, the black hole at the centre of our galaxy, 27,000 light years away (the Spacetime Lab shows what a black hole like it does). The <b>yellow rings</b> are Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, the two spacecraft that have left the Sun’s wind. Tap any of them for facts.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>What is to scale:</b> the directions and distances. The dots are markers, not sizes: at this scale even the Sun is less than a pixel. The Voyagers are at their real place on 30 Sep 2026 and then move along a straight line at their real speed, which is very close but not exact. Stars move slowly through space too, and that is ignored.</p>",
