  "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The stars in the background</b> (the <b>Soft</b> setting keeps them dim while you look at the planets and brightens them as you zoom out; <b>Full</b> keeps them bright) are real: the 2,874 brightest ones (about what your eyes see in a dark place), each in its true direction and true colour, with a soft band for the flat disc of our galaxy, the Milky Way (its real plane, drawn as a simple glow, not a photograph). They are so far away that they do not move when you zoom or follow something. They only move when you turn the picture.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Constellations</b> (in the Sky card) draws the lines people drew between stars long ago. The stars of a constellation are usually not near each other: they only look that way from here.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Far-away things:</b> zoom out a very long way (or tap a tile under Far away). The <b>white rings with a coloured dot</b> are real stars at their real direction and distance, like Alpha Centauri, the nearest star system, 4.3 light years away. The distance rings count light years too. The <b>blue rings</b> are the Pleiades and the Orion Nebula, and the <b>ring with a black dot</b> is Sagittarius A*, the black hole at the centre of our galaxy, 27,000 light years away (the Spacetime Lab shows what a black hole like it does). The <b>yellow rings</b> are Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, the two spacecraft that have left the Sun’s wind. Tap any of them for facts.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>What is to scale:</b> the directions and distances. The dots are markers, not sizes: at this scale even the Sun is less than a pixel. The Voyagers are at their real place on 30 Sep 2026 and then move along a straight line at their real speed, which is very close but not exact. Stars move slowly through space too, and that is ignored.</p>"};   // index = (3D ? 4 : 0) + (Easy to see ? 2 : 0) + (something selected ? 1 : 0)
struct TimelineRef { const char* y; const char* who; const char* t; const char* d; const char* button; const char* svg; };   // button: the ▶ button's label, or ""
static const TimelineRef kTimelineRef[] = {
  {"thousands of years ago", "Babylonians, Egyptians, Greeks, Chinese, Maya and many others", "Wandering stars",
   "Everyone noticed that five bright “stars” slowly wander through the constellations while all the others stay put: Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn. The Greek word for wanderer is <i>planetes</i>. Ancient astronomers wrote down their positions for centuries and learned to predict them.",
   "", "<circle cx=\"10\" cy=\"14\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/><circle cx=\"24\" cy=\"10\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"16\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"30\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/><circle cx=\"46\" cy=\"34\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/><path d=\"M8 46 Q24 30 48 22\" stroke=\"#d27a2a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\"/><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"34\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#d27a2a\"/>"},
  {"about 270–240 BC", "Aristarchus and Eratosthenes, Greece", "Sun in the middle, and the size of the Earth",
   "Aristarchus argued that the Earth goes around the Sun, but almost nobody believed him. Eratosthenes measured how long the shadow of a stick was in two Egyptian cities on the same day, and worked out the size of the Earth. His answer was within a few percent of the real 40,000 km.",
   "", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"14\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4a8ad2\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M28 28 L28 8 M28 28 L44 40\" stroke=\"#9aa5b8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"10\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#f0b020\"/>"},
  {"about 150 AD", "Claudius Ptolemy, Egypt", "Earth in the middle",
   "Ptolemy wrote the model that lasted 1,400 years: the Earth sits still in the centre, and each planet moves on a small circle (an epicycle) that rides on a bigger circle. It was complicated, but it predicted planet positions well enough for centuries.",
   "", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#4a8ad2\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"14\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\"/><circle cx=\"42\" cy=\"28\" r=\"6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d27a2a\"/><circle cx=\"48\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#d27a2a\"/>"},
  {"1543", "Nicolaus Copernicus, Poland", "The Sun in the centre",
   "Copernicus published a book that put the Sun in the middle and made the Earth one of the planets. It explained why planets seem to loop backwards in the sky: we overtake them on our faster, inner orbit. Many people still did not accept it for a hundred years.",
   "See the inner Solar System", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f0b020\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\"/><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#4a8ad2\"/>"},
  {"1609–1619", "Johannes Kepler, Germany (using Tycho Brahe’s data)", "Orbits are ellipses",
   "From years of Tycho’s precise observations of Mars, Kepler found three laws. Planets move on ellipses, not circles, with the Sun at one focus. They move faster when closer to the Sun. And a planet’s year squared is proportional to its distance cubed. Kepler could say how big the orbits were compared to each other, but not yet in kilometres.",
   "Look at Mars’s orbit", "<ellipse cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" rx=\"22\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#f0b020\"/><circle cx=\"46\" cy=\"27\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#d27a2a\"/>"},
  {"1610", "Galileo Galilei, Italy", "The telescope changes everything",
   "Galileo pointed a telescope at Jupiter and saw four small lights that changed places from night to night. They were moons going around Jupiter: proof that not everything goes around the Earth. He also saw that Venus has phases like the Moon, which only makes sense if Venus goes around the Sun.",
   "Watch Jupiter’s four big moons", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#c9a06a\"/><circle cx=\"10\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/><circle cx=\"42\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/><circle cx=\"48\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/>"},
  {"1655–1659", "Christiaan Huygens, Netherlands", "Saturn’s ring",
   "With a better telescope than Galileo’s, Huygens found Saturn’s biggest moon, Titan, in 1655. Fifty years earlier Galileo had seen odd “ears” on Saturn, and nobody could explain them. In 1659 Huygens did: Saturn is surrounded by a thin, flat ring that never touches the planet. The ring seems to vanish about every 15 years, when we see it edge-on.",
   "Look at Saturn’s rings", "<ellipse cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" rx=\"23\" ry=\"7\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8d4a8\" stroke-width=\"3\" transform=\"rotate(-18 28 28)\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#d8c28a\"/>"},
  {"1672–1769", "Cassini and Richer; then teams watching Venus cross the Sun", "How far is the Sun?",
   "Kepler gave the shape of the Solar System but not its size. Astronomers measured the direction of Mars from two far-apart places (1672), and later timed Venus crossing the face of the Sun from around the world (1761 and 1769). Result: the Sun is about 150 million km away. That distance is the AU used in this lab.",
   "", "<circle cx=\"14\" cy=\"28\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#f0b020\"/><circle cx=\"44\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#4a8ad2\"/><path d=\"M19 28 L40 28\" stroke=\"#9aa5b8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>"},
  {"1687", "Isaac Newton, England", "Gravity explains it all",
   "Newton showed that the same force that makes an apple fall also keeps the Moon and planets in orbit. Gravity gets weaker with the square of the distance, and from that alone all three of Kepler’s laws follow. This is the law that moves everything in this lab.",
   "", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"16\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#d24a4a\"/><path d=\"M28 22 L28 42\" stroke=\"#9aa5b8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M23 38 L28 44 L33 38\" stroke=\"#9aa5b8\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"50\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#4a8ad2\"/>"},
  {"1758", "Edmond Halley, England, and astronomers who checked it", "A comet comes back",
   "Halley worked out with Newton’s gravity that a comet seen in 1531, 1607 and 1682 was one comet returning every 76 years. He predicted it would come back in 1758. He had died by then, but it appeared on schedule, and the comet carries his name. It is the first proof that Newton’s law works far beyond the planets.",
   "", "<circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"36\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#cfd8ea\"/><path d=\"M19 34 Q34 22 50 12\" stroke=\"#cfd8ea\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\" opacity=\".6\"/><path d=\"M19 37 Q36 28 52 22\" stroke=\"#cfd8ea\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\" opacity=\".5\"/>"},
  {"1781–1846", "William Herschel, England; Urbain Le Verrier, France; Johann Galle, Germany", "Uranus and Neptune",
   "Herschel spotted Uranus in 1781, the first planet found with a telescope. Uranus wandered slightly off its predicted path, so Le Verrier used Newton’s gravity to calculate where a hidden planet must be. Galle pointed his telescope there in 1846 and found Neptune within one degree of the prediction. A planet found with a pen and paper.",
   "Look at Neptune", "<circle cx=\"22\" cy=\"28\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#4aa8c0\"/><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"28\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#3a5ad8\"/><path d=\"M30 12 L34 8 M32 14 L36 10\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\"/>"},
  {"1801", "Giuseppe Piazzi, Italy", "Ceres, the first asteroid",
   "Piazzi found a new “planet” exactly in the gap between Mars and Jupiter where astronomers thought one was missing. But Ceres was tiny. More and more little worlds turned up nearby, and Ceres was renamed an asteroid, and today a dwarf planet. This is the asteroid belt.",
   "Look at Ceres", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#9a9a94\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"20\" r=\"1.8\" fill=\"#7a7a74\"/><circle cx=\"44\" cy=\"18\" r=\"1.8\" fill=\"#7a7a74\"/><circle cx=\"46\" cy=\"38\" r=\"1.8\" fill=\"#7a7a74\"/><circle cx=\"14\" cy=\"40\" r=\"1.8\" fill=\"#7a7a74\"/>"},
  {"1918", "Harlow Shapley, USA", "The Sun is not in the middle of the galaxy",
   "Shapley mapped the globular clusters, big round balls of very old stars, and saw that they all sit around one far-off spot in the constellation Sagittarius, not around us. The centre of the Milky Way had to be there. He put it about 30,000 light years from the Sun. Today’s best measurement is about 27,000, so he was close. The Sun is an ordinary star in the suburbs of the galaxy.",
   "Fly to the centre of the galaxy", "<ellipse cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" rx=\"25\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"#cfd8ea\" opacity=\".2\"/><circle cx=\"22\" cy=\"27\" r=\"1.5\" fill=\"#8fd0ff\"/><circle cx=\"18\" cy=\"31\" r=\"1.5\" fill=\"#8fd0ff\"/><circle cx=\"24\" cy=\"23\" r=\"1.5\" fill=\"#8fd0ff\"/><circle cx=\"14\" cy=\"26\" r=\"1.5\" fill=\"#8fd0ff\"/><circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"27\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#fff\"/><circle cx=\"46\" cy=\"29\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#f0b020\"/>"},
  {"1930–2006", "Clyde Tombaugh, USA; the International Astronomical Union", "Pluto, and then not a planet",
   "Tombaugh found Pluto in 1930 and it was called the ninth planet. But in the 1990s we found that Pluto is just one of many icy bodies past Neptune, and in 2005 Eris was found, about the same size. In 2006 astronomers created a new class, dwarf planets, and Pluto joined it.",
   "Look at Pluto", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#c9a88a\"/><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"20\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#9a9a94\"/><path d=\"M8 40 Q28 50 50 36\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\" fill=\"none\"/>"},
  {"1957–1969", "Soviet Union and USA", "The Space Age",
   "Sputnik was the first artificial satellite in 1957. Yuri Gagarin was the first human in space in 1961, and Mariner 2 flew past Venus in 1962, the first successful visit to another planet. In 1969, Apollo 11 put people on the Moon.",
   "", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#4a8ad2\"/><ellipse cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9aa5b8\" transform=\"rotate(-25 28 28)\"/><circle cx=\"46\" cy=\"20\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#fff\"/>"},
  {"1970–1976", "Soviet Union’s Venera 7; NASA’s Viking 1", "Landing on Venus and Mars",
   "Venera 7 landed on Venus on 15 December 1970, the first spacecraft to land on another planet and send data back from its surface. It measured about 475 °C, and it lasted only a short while in that heat and crushing air. On 20 July 1976 Viking 1 became the first Mars lander to work properly. It kept going for more than six years, took photos, measured the weather, and ran experiments that searched the soil for signs of life. The answers were unclear.",
   "Jump to the day Viking 1 landed", "<circle cx=\"18\" cy=\"28\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#d9a441\"/><circle cx=\"42\" cy=\"28\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#d0553a\"/><path d=\"M18 39 L18 45 M14 45 L22 45\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><path d=\"M42 36 L42 42 M38 42 L46 42\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/>"},
  {"1977–1989", "NASA, USA", "Voyager’s Grand Tour",
   "Voyager 1 and 2 launched in 1977, in a year when Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune lined up, which happens about once every 175 years. Voyager 2 used gravity assists to fly past Jupiter (1979), Saturn (1981), Uranus (1986) and Neptune (1989). It is still the only spacecraft that has visited Uranus and Neptune.",
   "Try the slingshot game", "<circle cx=\"10\" cy=\"44\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#4a8ad2\"/><circle cx=\"22\" cy=\"22\" r=\"4.5\" fill=\"#c9a06a\"/><circle cx=\"36\" cy=\"30\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#d8c07a\"/><circle cx=\"48\" cy=\"14\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#3a5ad8\"/><path d=\"M10 44 Q16 22 22 22 Q30 24 36 30 Q44 22 48 14\" stroke=\"#e0b020\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>"},
  {"1992", "David Jewitt and Jane Luu, USA", "The Kuiper belt",
   "Astronomers had guessed for decades that a belt of icy bodies lay beyond Neptune. In 1992 Jewitt and Luu found the first one. Now over a thousand are known, and Pluto turns out to be one of the largest of them.",
   "Look at the Kuiper belt", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#f0b020\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"22\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\" stroke-dasharray=\"1 3\"/><circle cx=\"10\" cy=\"20\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/><circle cx=\"44\" cy=\"12\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"34\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/><circle cx=\"24\" cy=\"50\" r=\"1.6\" fill=\"#9aa5b8\"/>"},
  {"1994", "Carolyn and Eugene Shoemaker and David Levy, USA and Canada; astronomers everywhere", "A comet hits Jupiter",
   "Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9 was found in March 1993 already torn into a “string of pearls” of more than 20 pieces, after passing close to Jupiter in 1992. Between 16 and 22 July 1994, 21 of the pieces crashed into Jupiter one after another. It was the first time people saw two bodies in the Solar System collide. The biggest left a dark scar nearly as wide as the whole Earth.",
   "Jump to the first impact, 16 July 1994", "<circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"32\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#c9a06a\"/><circle cx=\"6\" cy=\"8\" r=\"1.4\" fill=\"#cfd8ea\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"13\" r=\"1.7\" fill=\"#cfd8ea\"/><circle cx=\"18\" cy=\"18\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#cfd8ea\"/><circle cx=\"24\" cy=\"23\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#cfd8ea\"/><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#cfd8ea\"/>"},
  {"1995", "Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz, Switzerland", "Other suns have planets",
   "The first planet around a Sun-like star, 51 Pegasi b, was found in 1995. It was a giant planet closer to its star than Mercury is to ours. Since then thousands more have been found, and the Solar System turned out to be just one kind of planetary system. Mayor and Queloz won the 2019 Nobel Prize in Physics.",
   "", "<circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"28\" r=\"7\" fill=\"#f0b020\"/><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"28\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#c9a06a\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"28\" r=\"18\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 3\" opacity=\".6\"/>"},
  {"2004–2005", "NASA and ESA, USA and Europe", "Landing on Titan",
   "The Cassini spacecraft reached Saturn in 2004, and in January 2005 the Huygens probe parachuted down onto Titan, Saturn’s largest moon. It was the first landing in the outer Solar System. Titan has clouds, rivers and lakes, but of methane, not water.",
   "Look at Saturn and Titan", "<circle cx=\"22\" cy=\"28\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d8c07a\"/><ellipse cx=\"22\" cy=\"28\" rx=\"16\" ry=\"4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d8c07a\" opacity=\".7\"/><circle cx=\"44\" cy=\"20\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#d27a2a\"/>"},
  {"2012", "NASA’s Voyager 1", "Leaving the Sun’s wind",
   "On 25 August 2012 Voyager 1 passed the heliopause, the edge of the bubble blown by the Sun’s wind, 121 AU from the Sun. It was the first spacecraft to enter interstellar space, though NASA only confirmed it in September 2013. Voyager 2 followed in 2018, at 119 AU.",
   "Jump to that day and fly out to Voyager 1", "<circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"36\" r=\"16\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9aa5b8\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/><circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"36\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#f0b020\"/><path d=\"M26 31 L45 15\" stroke=\"#ffd27a\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\"/><circle cx=\"47\" cy=\"13\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#fff\"/><circle cx=\"47\" cy=\"13\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ffd27a\"/>"},
  {"2014–2015", "ESA’s Rosetta and Philae; NASA’s Dawn", "Landing on a comet, and orbiting Ceres",
   "On 12 November 2014 Rosetta’s small lander Philae touched down on comet 67P/Churyumov–Gerasimenko, the first landing on a comet. It bounced twice and came to rest in the shade, so its battery ran out after about three days. In March 2015 Dawn arrived at Ceres, the first spacecraft to orbit a dwarf planet, and the first ever to orbit two different worlds, since it had already circled the asteroid Vesta. Ceres has bright spots in a crater called Occator.",
   "Jump to Philae’s landing day", "<circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"38\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#9a9a94\"/><path d=\"M12 35 Q4 26 2 18\" stroke=\"#cfd8ea\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\" opacity=\".6\"/><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"20\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#8a8a86\"/><circle cx=\"43\" cy=\"18\" r=\"1.7\" fill=\"#fff\"/><circle cx=\"38\" cy=\"23\" r=\"1.3\" fill=\"#fff\"/>"},
  {"2015", "NASA’s New Horizons, USA", "Pluto up close",
   "After nine years on its way, New Horizons flew past Pluto at 14 km/s on 14 July 2015. Pluto turned out to have mountains of ice, a huge heart-shaped plain, and thin blue haze. It kept going and passed a Kuiper belt rock named Arrokoth in 2019.",
   "", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#c9a88a\"/><path d=\"M22 24 Q28 18 34 24 Q28 36 22 24 Z\" fill=\"#efe0d0\"/>"},
  {"2017", "Pan-STARRS telescope, Hawaii", "A visitor from another star",
   "ʻOumuamua was the first object ever seen passing through the Solar System from interstellar space. It moved too fast for the Sun to hold it, and its path was not a closed ellipse. A second one, the comet Borisov, was found in 2019.",
   "", "<path d=\"M6 46 Q30 30 52 8\" stroke=\"#e0b020\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#e0b020\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#f0b020\"/>"},
  {"2018–2021", "NASA’s Parker Solar Probe, USA", "Touching the Sun",
   "Parker launched on 12 August 2018 to go closer to the Sun than any spacecraft before. On 28 April 2021 it flew into the corona, the Sun’s thin outer atmosphere, the first spacecraft to do so (announced that December). On 24 December 2024 it passed about 6.2 million km above the Sun’s surface at about 690,000 km/h, the fastest any human-made object has ever travelled. Its path is not drawn in the lab.",
   "Look at the Sun", "<circle cx=\"14\" cy=\"42\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#f0b020\"/><path d=\"M40 18 Q30 28 25 38\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\" fill=\"none\"/><circle cx=\"41\" cy=\"17\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#fff\"/>"},
  {"2020", "Reinhard Genzel, Germany, and Andrea Ghez, USA (Nobel Prize, shared with Roger Penrose)", "Weighing the black hole at the centre of our galaxy",
   "Since the early 1990s two teams followed stars near the centre of the Milky Way. One star, S2, goes round in about 16 years and swings within about 120 AU of the centre at 7,650 km/s. From orbits like that they found about four million Suns packed into a space smaller than the Solar System: a black hole, Sagittarius A*. Genzel and Ghez shared the 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics for it.",
   "Fly to Sagittarius A*", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#fff\"/><ellipse cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" rx=\"21\" ry=\"9\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8fd0ff\" transform=\"rotate(-30 28 28)\"/><circle cx=\"44\" cy=\"19\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#ffd27a\"/>"},
  {"2022", "NASA’s DART mission, USA", "Moving an asteroid",
   "DART crashed on purpose into a small asteroid moon called Dimorphos on 26 September 2022. Its orbit around its partner got 32 minutes shorter. It was the first time humans have changed the motion of a natural object in space, a test for protecting Earth one day.",
   "", "<circle cx=\"34\" cy=\"28\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#9a9a94\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"28\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#fff\"/><path d=\"M14 28 L24 28\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\"/>"},
  {"Today", "Astronomers everywhere", "And it is still going",
   "We know the planets, moons, asteroids and comets very well now, but the outer edge is still a mystery. The Oort cloud is a huge sphere of icy bodies that no one has ever directly seen, reaching maybe halfway to the nearest star. Somebody will find out what else is out there. Maybe you.",
   "Zoom out to the Oort cloud", "<circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#f0b020\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"10\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a86a0\"/><circle cx=\"28\" cy=\"28\" r=\"24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9aa5b8\" stroke-dasharray=\"1 4\"/>"}};
